---
/**
 * /evals — the product page.
 *
 * REBUILT ON 2026-08-13, replacing the version that had held this route since the site was
 * repositioned onto the four pillars. It was reviewed at /evals-v2 first and took over the
 * route once it was signed off; the page it replaced is in git history, and what follows
 * records what changed so a revert is a decision rather than an accident.
 *
 * WHAT CHANGED, and why. The old page was ordered claim, coverage, explanation, architecture,
 * report, install — a feature tour. This one is ordered outcome, proof, how it works, report,
 * locality, coverage, close, which follows the order the questions actually arrive in: what do
 * I get, is it safe to run on my repository, does it support my stack. The report moved ahead
 * of coverage and architecture because it is the most tangible product proof on the page and
 * nothing is served by making a reader walk past integrations to reach it.
 *
 * EVERY SECTION BELOW THE HERO SHARES ONE SHELL, which is the change that does the most work.
 * Each one is an eyebrow, a statement, a short paragraph, exactly one main object and a quiet
 * evidence footer, at one set of widths, one border weight and one radius — .ev-mod, .ev-copy,
 * .ev-obj, .ev-foot. Before that each section had invented its own grammar: logo pills here,
 * an architecture diagram there, a fake browser window below it, and three different footer
 * treatments. /market reads as one page because its charts are exhibits in one museum, and
 * these were exhibits borrowed from three.
 *
 * Four substantive changes beyond the ordering:
 *
 *   A REAL RESULT SITS SECOND. The old page argued that cost per completed task is the
 *   metric and shows no figure. This one shows three routes out of the published example run
 *   with the measures that disagree with each other, which is the whole argument in one
 *   table. Every number comes from src/data/example-run.json, which
 *   scripts/extract-example-run.mjs reads out of the report the page links to, so the page
 *   and the artefact cannot drift.
 *
 *   THE ROUTES ARE LETTERED RATHER THAN NAMED, and this is the page's one contested
 *   decision. /market names hosts in a cost ranking as a deliberate call; the same call comes
 *   out the other way here. The run is one local run on one repository from 29 July, its
 *   archive file in fantastic-dollop is set aside, and the route with the lowest completion
 *   count has a slowest step close to the runner's own session timeout — which
 *   outreach/results/README.md names as the specific trap that produces a false failure about
 *   a named company. The report one click away names all ten.
 *
 *   COVERAGE IS GENERATED. The old page hard-coded eleven logos and claimed "every router,
 *   every direct provider" over a registry that carries three routers and fourteen direct
 *   APIs. Both counts and both rows now come from src/data/adapters.json, extracted from
 *   ../canaryone/src/proxy/providers.ts.
 *
 *   THE LOCALITY SECTION STATES THE OUTBOUND TRUTH. "No dashboard, no login, no cloud" on
 *   the old page was contradicted by its own architecture diagram two sections earlier, and
 *   the "no cloud" half is inside the claim family COPY.md freezes and tools/x_post.py
 *   blocks outright.
 *
 * WHAT WENT. The hub-and-spoke provider tree, which drew CanaryOne as a gateway sitting in
 * the request path — the one thing the page spends a section promising it is not — along with
 * the client script that measured it. The standalone "route matters" section, which is
 * repetition once a measured result leads. The receipt import and the lightbox, which were
 * already dead: the old page computed `receiptFull` and looked up a `#receipt-frame` that no
 * longer existed in its markup. The browser chrome around the report — traffic-light dots and a
 * fake URL bar — which was quoting Safari to borrow credibility the artefact already has.
 * And the two full-width screenshots stacked on top of each other, which said there were three
 * product views and then showed a scroll of several at once.
 *
 * The copy-button script is the only JavaScript here, and it is the home page's existing
 * install component. The report's three views switch with no script at all: hidden radios,
 * labels as chips, and one generated rule per view, which is the pattern /market uses for its
 * model selector.
 */
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Layout, { GITHUB_URL } from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import viewLeaderboard from '../assets/report-view-leaderboard.png';
import viewHeatmap from '../assets/report-view-heatmap.png';
import viewSession from '../assets/report-view-session.png';
import run from '../data/example-run.json';
import adapters from '../data/adapters.json';
import market from '../data/market.json';
import { spell, cap, times, day, usd4, secs } from '../lib/prose';

const TITLE = 'CanaryOne — find the route that wins on your workload';
const DESCRIPTION =
  'Run the tests already in your repository against the same model across routers and direct providers. Compare completion, cost per completed task and latency before you move production traffic.';

const DEMO_REPORT = run.reportUrl;
const QUICKSTART = `${GITHUB_URL}#first-run-30-seconds`;

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The three routes the proof table shows.

   Chosen by rule rather than by hand, so a re-extracted run cannot leave the page pointing at
   a lane that no longer exists or quietly keep the old story. The rule is the argument: the
   cheapest route, the cheapest route that finished most of the work, and the only route that
   finished all of it. Lettered by cost, cheapest first.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
type Lane = (typeof run.lanes)[number];

const byCost = (a: Lane, b: Lane) => a.costPerPass - b.costPerPass;
const RELIABLE = 0.75;                       /* completed at least three quarters of attempts */

const cheapest = [...run.lanes].sort(byCost)[0];
const reliable = [...run.lanes]
  .filter((l) => l.passed / l.attempted >= RELIABLE && l.slug !== cheapest.slug)
  .sort(byCost)[0];
const complete = [...run.lanes]
  .filter((l) => l.slug !== cheapest.slug && l.slug !== reliable.slug)
  .sort((a, b) => b.passed - a.passed || a.costPerPass - b.costPerPass)[0];

/* The letter is positional, so it is assigned after the sort rather than carried along with
   each lane. Three columns and no more: the report holds all ten. */
const shown = [cheapest, reliable, complete].sort(byCost);
const LETTERS = ['Route A', 'Route B', 'Route C'];

/* The spread the section leads with: dearest of the three over cheapest, per completed task. */
const costRatio = shown[shown.length - 1].costPerPass / shown[0].costPerPass;
/* And the gap between the cheapest route and the cheapest one that finished most of the work,
   which is the sentence that makes "cheapest" stop meaning "cheapest to finish". */
const reliablePremium = reliable.costPerPass / cheapest.costPerPass;
/* times() renders a ratio under two as "46 per cent above", which is right in a comparison of
   two named things and stilted in "cost 46 per cent above". This is the plainer phrasing for a
   sentence whose referent is the row above it. */
const pctMore = (r: number) => `${Math.round((r - 1) * 100)} per cent more`;

/* The runner stops a session at six minutes, and the caveat below needs that number in
   minutes beside a slowest step measured in milliseconds. Held here rather than inline
   because it is a fact about the tool, not about this run. */
const SESSION_TIMEOUT_MIN = 6;
const slowestStepMin = Math.max(...run.lanes.map((l) => l.p95Ms)) / 60000;

/* ---- the nightly sweep, as the second and reproducible half of the same finding ----
   The example run is a local run and carries demonstration material only. The sweep is the
   commissioned measurement in timeseries/, it repeats every night, and it is where the
   listed-price half of the claim comes from — the same figure /market leads with. */
const m = market.measured;
const pair = m?.same_price_pair;
const sweepRatio = pair ? pair.ratio : 0;
const sweepListedGap = pair ? Math.round((1 - pair.dear_listed / pair.cheap_listed) * 100) : 0;

/* ---- how the run fanned out, for the hero ----
   Router counts rather than company names: the hero's middle panel is saying "the same tests,
   replayed this many ways", and naming companies there would put a name a column away from a
   completion count in the panel beside it. */
const byRouter = run.lanes.reduce((acc: Record<string, number>, l) => {
  acc[l.router] = (acc[l.router] ?? 0) + 1;
  return acc;
}, {});
const routerRows = Object.entries(byRouter).sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1]);
/* Every lane in this run tested the same weights under four provider-native slugs. The short
   name is what a reader knows the model as. */
const MODEL = 'Kimi K3';

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Coverage, read off the shipped registry.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* A CHIP IS A SQUARE MARK PLUS THE COMPANY'S NAME IN OUR OWN TYPE, and both halves of that
   are the fix for the same problem. Wordmark lockups were tried first and seventeen brands
   drawn in eleven different typefaces made the row the busiest block on the page, while the
   six adapters with no lockup in the library came out as a visibly second tier. The marks are
   monochrome and take one ink, so the row reads as one system, and the name beside each one is
   what keeps it readable as a list of companies rather than a wall of glyphs — DESIGN.md's
   objection to a bare-mark row is that it cannot be read as names, and a labelled mark can.
   Which mark belongs to which adapter is decided by the extractor, not here. */

/* NO PER-ADAPTER STATE MARKER, and that is a decision rather than an omission. An earlier
   version put a filled dot on the six adapters appearing in the example run and a hollow one on
   the rest, which is the FILLED MEANS PRESENT idiom /market uses. It cost more than it bought
   here: a second sentence of key in the note under the row, on a section whose job is to say
   that the coverage is broad. The row's own label carries the relationship, which is what
   COPY.md asks a logo row for, and the run that names its own six routes is one section above. */
const shippedRouters = adapters.routers.filter((r) => r.shipped);
const shippedDirect = adapters.direct.filter((d) => d.shipped);

/* ONE LINE PER CELL, which is what keeps the grid dense. Two adapters share one mark, because
   Moonshot ships an international and a mainland endpoint, and the registry distinguishes them
   in a parenthetical: "Moonshot AI (intl)". Rendered whole that needs two lines and doubles
   every cell's height across the whole grid for the sake of two of them.
   So a parenthetical collapses into the name — "Moonshot Intl" — by rule rather than by a map
   of hand-written short names, which would be another list to keep in step with the registry.
   Everything else already fits a cell at its registry name. */
const REGION_SHORT: Record<string, string> = { intl: 'Intl', cn: 'CN' };
const cellName = (name: string) => {
  const m = name.match(/^(.*?)\s*\(([^)]+)\)$/);
  if (!m) return name;
  const stem = m[1].split(' ')[0];
  return `${stem} ${REGION_SHORT[m[2]] ?? m[2]}`;
};

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The report's three views.

   ONE VIEW AT A TIME, which is the whole point of the rebuild. The section names three
   distinct things the report gives you and then used to show two full-width screenshots
   stacked, so the eye read three descriptions followed by an undifferentiated scroll. Each
   view is now a crop of exactly the thing its label names, and switching is a radio.

   The crops are screenshots of public/demo-report/index.html — the same artefact the section
   links to — taken at twice the display width so they stay sharp. The descriptions are
   deliberately parallel and start on a verb, because three descriptions of three views should
   read as one set.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
const views = [
  {
    num: '01',
    name: 'Leaderboard',
    blurb: 'Rank every route by cost per completed task, with completion, judge score and latency beside it.',
    img: viewLeaderboard,
    alt: 'The lane table from a CanaryOne report: one row per route, with completion, cost per pass, judge score, weighted cost per pass and latency percentiles.',
  },
  {
    num: '02',
    name: 'Task heatmap',
    blurb: 'See exactly which tasks each route failed, rather than only how often it failed.',
    img: viewHeatmap,
    alt: 'The heatmap from a CanaryOne report: one row per route, one column per task, each cell carrying that cell’s cost and shaded by it.',
  },
  {
    num: '03',
    name: 'Session drill-down',
    blurb: 'Read the judge’s verdict on a single attempt, its four sub-scores and the test’s own output.',
    img: viewSession,
    alt: 'One expanded session from a CanaryOne report: cost, duration and step count, four judge sub-scores, the judge’s written reasoning, and the tail of the test process’s output.',
  },
];

/* One rule set per view, pairing radio N with chip N and card N. Generated because the count is
   data-driven, and injected globally because Astro's scoped styles cannot carry a generated
   block. Every selector sits under .ev-sel, which exists only on this page.
   The card step is a descendant of .ev-obj rather than a sibling of the radio: the cards live
   inside the panel, so the chain is sibling to the panel and then down into it. */
const viewCss = views.map((_v, i) => `
.ev-sel > input:nth-of-type(${i + 1}):checked ~ .ev-obj .ev-cards > :nth-child(${i + 1}) {
  display: block;
}
.ev-sel > input:nth-of-type(${i + 1}):checked ~ .ev-rail > label:nth-child(${i + 1}) {
  background: var(--accent); border-color: var(--accent-deep); color: var(--dark);
  font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
}
.ev-sel > input:nth-of-type(${i + 1}):focus-visible ~ .ev-rail > label:nth-child(${i + 1}) {
  outline: var(--focus-width) solid var(--focus-color); outline-offset: var(--focus-offset);
}`).join('');

/* The report's own identity line, in place of the browser chrome that used to sit here. Four
   facts about the run, which is what a reader needs to know before reading a figure off it. */
const runBar = [
  `run ${run.runId.split('-')[0]}`,
  MODEL,
  `${run.totals.lanes} routes`,
  `${run.totals.tasks} tasks`,
].join(' · ');
---

<Layout title={TITLE} description={DESCRIPTION} path="/evals">
  <!-- ─── 1. HERO ───
       The decision, not the mechanism. The visual is a live piece of the product rather than
       an illustration: the repository on the left, the fan-out in the middle, what it cost to
       finish on the right. Every figure in it comes from the published example run. -->
  <div class="page-band">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="page-head">
        <p class="kicker">WORKLOAD BENCHMARKING</p>
        <h1>Find the route that wins on your workload.</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Run the tests already in your repository against the same model across supported
          routers and direct providers. Compare completion, cost per completed task and
          latency before you move production traffic.
        </p>

        <div class="ev-ctas">
          <a class="btn primary" href="#install">Benchmark my workload →</a>
          <a class="btn ghost" href={DEMO_REPORT} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            <span>View an example report</span>
          </a>
        </div>
        <p class="ev-trust">
          Runs on your machine · static HTML report · no CanaryOne account
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="ev-flow">
        <div class="ev-stage">
          <p class="ev-stage-label">Your repository</p>
          <div class="ev-card">
            <p class="ev-card-title">your-repo/</p>
            <dl class="ev-kv">
              <div><dt>tests that call an LLM</dt><dd>{run.totals.tasks}</dd></div>
              <div><dt>model under test</dt><dd>{MODEL}</dd></div>
              <div><dt>repeats each</dt><dd>{run.totals.repeats}</dd></div>
            </dl>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="ev-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</p>

        <div class="ev-stage">
          <p class="ev-stage-label">The same tests, every route</p>
          <div class="ev-card">
            <p class="ev-card-title">{run.totals.lanes} routes</p>
            <dl class="ev-kv">
              {routerRows.map(([router, n]) => (
                <div><dt>{router}</dt><dd>{n}</dd></div>
              ))}
            </dl>
            <p class="ev-card-foot">{run.totals.sessions} sessions · {run.wallClock}</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="ev-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</p>

        <div class="ev-stage">
          <p class="ev-stage-label">What it cost to finish</p>
          <div class="ev-card">
            <table class="ev-mini">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">route</th>
                  <th scope="col">done</th>
                  <th scope="col">$ / done</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {shown.map((l, i) => (
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="row">{LETTERS[i]}</th>
                    <td>{l.passed}/{l.attempted}</td>
                    <td>{usd4(l.costPerPass)}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
            <p class="ev-card-foot">
              <a href={DEMO_REPORT} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
                and {run.totals.lanes - shown.length} more in the full report →
              </a>
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ─── 2. PROOF ───
       The strongest thing on the page, moved to second. It is not an argument that routes
       differ; it is three routes differing, with the run one click away. -->
  <section class="section ev-mod">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="ev-copy">
        <p class="kicker">MEASURED WITH CANARYONE</p>
        <h2>Token price is not<br /><span class="em">workload cost.</span></h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Buy the same model several different ways and it does not do the same work for the
          same money. Three routes out of one run below, on identical tasks and identical
          weights, with the measures a price list cannot show you.
        </p>
        <p class="ev-claim">
          The lowest-cost route completed only {spell(cheapest.passed)} of
          {' '}{spell(cheapest.attempted)} attempts.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="ev-obj ev-table-obj">
        <table class="tm-data-table ev-compare">
          <caption class="sr-only">
            Three of the {run.totals.lanes} routes measured in run {run.runId.split('-')[0]},
            showing attempts completed, cost per completed task, judge score and slowest step.
          </caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">On this workload</th>
              {shown.map((_l, i) => <th scope="col">{LETTERS[i]}</th>)}
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Attempts completed</th>
              {shown.map((l) => <td>{l.passed} of {l.attempted}</td>)}
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Cost per completed task</th>
              {shown.map((l) => <td>{usd4(l.costPerPass)}</td>)}
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Judge score, out of 100</th>
              {shown.map((l) => <td>{l.judge}</td>)}
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Slowest step</th>
              {shown.map((l) => <td>{secs(l.p95Ms)}</td>)}
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="ev-foot">
        <p class="ev-src">
          Run <code>{run.runId.split('-')[0]}</code> · {day(run.startedAt)} ·
          {' '}{run.totals.lanes} routes · {run.totals.tasks} tasks ·
          {' '}{run.totals.repeats} repeats each ·
          {' '}<a href={DEMO_REPORT} target="_blank" rel="noopener">open the full report →</a>
        </p>

        {/* THE CONCLUSION, AND ONE CAVEAT. The rest went behind the disclosure below.
            This footer carried a nine-line paragraph holding the conclusion, the spread, an
            explanation of the judge and two caveats, and it made the strongest section on the
            page the heaviest thing to read. The claim line above the table opens the argument;
            this closes it. */}
        <p class="ev-note">
          The cheapest route that completed most of the work cost
          {' '}{pctMore(reliablePremium)}.
        </p>
        <p class="ev-caveat">
          This is one measured run, not a claim about any provider.
        </p>

        <details class="ev-disclose">
          <summary>How to read this result</summary>
          <div class="ev-disclose-body">
            <p>
              Across the three routes, cost per completed task runs {times(costRatio)} from one
              end to the other on identical work.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>The judge score.</strong> A second model reads each session transcript and
              scores the work out of a hundred. A high cost beside a high score is a route doing
              the job thoroughly; a low score beside a pass is a test that succeeded on an answer
              the model narrated rather than grounded in what its tools returned.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>The timeout.</strong> The runner stops a session after
              {' '}{spell(SESSION_TIMEOUT_MIN)} minutes, and the slowest step in this run ran to
              {' '}{spell(Math.round(slowestStepMin))} minutes. Some of what the lowest-cost route
              did not finish may be that timeout rather than the route.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Why the routes are lettered.</strong> One run of one tool against one
              repository on {day(run.startedAt)} demonstrates what the measurement produces; it
              does not establish anything about a company. The size of a gap like this repeats
              from run to run and which route sits at either end does not, so the magnitude is
              the finding. The report names all {spell(run.totals.lanes)}.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        {/* The reproducible half of the same finding, as a citation rather than a second
            paragraph of argument. Guarded the way /market guards it: `measured` is regenerated
            from the nightly sweep and a failed night leaves it absent, which should drop the
            note rather than fail the build. */}
        {pair && (
        <p class="ev-evidence">
          <span class="ev-evidence-label">
            Also measured · {day(`${m.night}T00:00:00Z`)}
          </span>
          On the nightly sweep, the route with the cheaper advertised token price cost
          {' '}{times(sweepRatio)} as much per completed task, and both routes completed every
          task. {cap(spell(m.routes.length))} routes of {m.model_name}.
          {' '}<a href="/market">See the market page →</a>
        </p>
        )}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <!-- ─── 3. HOW IT WORKS ───
       Stated rather than inferred. The old page jumped from its headline into provider
       coverage, so a reader met eleven logos before knowing what the machine does. -->
  <section class="section ev-mod">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="ev-copy">
        <p class="kicker">HOW IT WORKS</p>
        <h2>Your tests in.<br />Comparable routes out.</h2>
      </div>

      <ol class="ev-steps">
        <li>
          <p class="ev-step-num">01</p>
          <h3>Point CanaryOne at your repository</h3>
          <p>
            It runs against any repository whose tests or eval harness call an LLM, using the
            tests you already trust rather than a benchmark you have to adopt.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="ev-step-num">02</p>
          <h3>Replay them across routes</h3>
          <p>
            The same tests and the same model weights go through each router and direct
            provider you configure. Your test code does not change; only the route does.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="ev-step-num">03</p>
          <h3>Compare the outcome</h3>
          <p>
            One report carries completion, cost per completed task, latency and the
            task-by-task failures underneath them.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── 4. THE REPORT ───
       Moved ahead of coverage and locality: it is the most tangible thing on the page, and a
       reader who has just seen a measured result wants to see what they get before they ask
       whether it is safe to run or whether it supports their stack.

       One view at a time, chosen from a rail of three. The headline was "A static HTML report,
       dropped in your repo", which made an implementation detail the star — static HTML is a
       good property and a bad benefit, so it moved to the footer. -->
  <section class="section ev-mod">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="ev-copy">
        <p class="kicker">THE REPORT</p>
        <h2>See why one route won.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Every run writes a self-contained report showing how each route performed across your
          actual tasks, so a decision comes with its own working.
        </p>
      </div>

      {/* A fieldset because that is what a group of radios is. min-inline-size is the
          load-bearing line — a fieldset defaults to min-content width and refuses to shrink
          below its widest child, which on a phone is a 1100px screenshot. */}
      <fieldset class="ev-sel">
        <legend class="sr-only">Report view</legend>
        {views.map((v, i) => (
          <input class="sr-only" type="radio" name="ev-view" id={`ev-v-${v.num}`}
                 checked={i === 0} />
        ))}

        <div class="ev-rail">
          {views.map((v) => (
            <label for={`ev-v-${v.num}`}>
              <span class="ev-rail-num">{v.num}</span> {v.name}
            </label>
          ))}
        </div>

        <div class="ev-obj">
          {/* The run's identity, in place of the browser chrome that used to sit here. Four
              facts a reader needs before taking a figure off the image behind it. */}
          <div class="ev-obj-bar">
            <p class="ev-obj-id">{runBar}</p>
            <a href={DEMO_REPORT} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open example report →</a>
          </div>

          <div class="ev-cards">
            {views.map((v) => (
              <div>
                <p class="ev-view-blurb">{v.blurb}</p>
                <Image src={v.img} alt={v.alt} widths={[760, 1100, v.img.width]}
                       sizes="min(1100px, 88vw)" loading="lazy" class="ev-view-img" />
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>
      </fieldset>

      <div class="ev-foot">
        <p class="ev-src">
          Saved as <code>&lt;repo&gt;/.c1/runs/&lt;runId&gt;/report/index.html</code> — one
          static file with no external requests, so it opens from disk and travels in a pull
          request.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── 5. RUNS ON YOUR MACHINE ───
       Rewritten for credibility rather than for reassurance. The old page said "nothing sits
       in your request path" and then "no dashboard, no login, no cloud" two sections later,
       while its own diagram showed the judge and the scan summariser leaving the machine. The
       "no cloud" half is in the claim family COPY.md freezes and x_post.py blocks.

       THE BOUNDARY IS THE DESIGN. The previous version gave one simple idea five treatments at
       once: a dashed container, a tint inside it, white cards inside that, a full-width slab
       for the outbound line, and a tagline under the lot. Now there is one panel, and the story
       is that one arrow leaves it. Inside is a surface; outside is the page.

       The crossing is the only yellow in the section, and it is a fill behind dark text rather
       than a coloured line, because on cream --accent-deep measures 1.87:1 and DESIGN.md rules
       out a yellow rule that has to be seen. A yellow pill on the boundary puts the accent
       exactly where the crossing happens and still passes. -->
  <section class="section ev-mod">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="ev-copy">
        <p class="kicker">WHAT LEAVES</p>
        <h2>Runs on your machine.<br />See exactly what leaves.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          The runner, the per-route proxy, the SQLite database and the generated report all stay
          on your machine, and nothing sits in your request path.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="ev-machine">
        <div class="ev-obj ev-inside">
          <p class="ev-inside-label">Your machine</p>
          <div class="ev-chain">
            <div class="ev-node">
              <p class="ev-node-name">Your tests</p>
              <p class="ev-node-sub">the harness you already have</p>
            </div>
            <span class="ev-chain-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
            <div class="ev-node">
              <p class="ev-node-name">CanaryOne proxy</p>
              <p class="ev-node-sub">one per route, on localhost</p>
            </div>
            <span class="ev-chain-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
            <div class="ev-node">
              <p class="ev-node-name">SQLite + report</p>
              <p class="ev-node-sub">under &lt;repo&gt;/.c1</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="ev-cross">
          <span class="ev-cross-pill">outbound only</span>
        </div>

        <div class="ev-outside">
          <p class="ev-outside-label">Leaves your machine</p>
          <dl class="ev-egress">
            <div>
              <dt>Model requests</dt>
              <dd>the providers you configure</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Session transcripts</dt>
              <dd>
                the judge, to be scored — optional, and
                {' '}<code>--disable-judge</code> turns it off
              </dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Your test files</dt>
              <dd>the scan summariser, which works out which of them call an LLM</dd>
            </div>
          </dl>
        </div>
      </div>

      <ul class="ev-badges">
        <li>No hosted dashboard</li>
        <li>No CanaryOne account</li>
        <li>The report stays in your repository</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="ev-foot">
        <details class="ev-disclose">
          <summary>Exactly what leaves your machine</summary>
          <div class="ev-disclose-body">
            <p>
              <strong>Model traffic.</strong> Every LLM call your test makes is rewritten for the
              route under test and forwarded to that provider's own endpoint. The full request
              and response log is written to
              {' '}<code>&lt;repo&gt;/.c1/runs/&lt;runId&gt;/traffic.jsonl</code>, so what was
              sent is on disk and readable.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>The judge.</strong> When judging is on, each finished session's transcript
              is sent to a gateway to be scored out of a hundred by a second model.
              {' '}<code>--disable-judge</code> turns it off, and the run then reports completion
              and cost with no quality score.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>The scan summariser.</strong> Working out which of your tests call an LLM
              sends those test files to the same gateway to be classified.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Your keys.</strong> They live in <code>~/.c1/.env</code> at mode
              {' '}<code>0600</code> and go to each provider's own endpoint and nowhere else. An
              OpenRouter key is required, because the judge and the model catalogue both run
              through it.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── 6. COVERAGE ───
       Counts and cells both generated. "Every router, every direct provider" came off: the
       registry carries three and fourteen, and "every" is inflation in the one place a
       technical reader is deciding whether to trust the numbers above.

       The numbers now lead, so nobody has to count logos to learn the breadth, and the marks
       sit in two fixed grids where every cell is the same size — the previous version sized
       each pill to its own name, which made two ragged rows out of one list. -->
  <section class="section ev-mod">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="ev-copy">
        <p class="kicker">COVERAGE</p>
        <h2>Across routers and direct providers.</h2>
        <p class="ev-stat">
          {adapters.counts.routers} routing platforms · {adapters.counts.direct} direct APIs
        </p>
        <p class="lede">
          Benchmark the same model through a gateway or straight against a provider's own
          endpoint, and measure both the same way.
        </p>
      </div>

      {/* Two rows, both dense, and the cell is the same component in each: a mark and a name on
          one line. The routers are three across directly under the copy; the fourteen providers
          are five across, which lands them in three rows rather than four.
          Both rows keep their own label because COPY.md requires a logo row to say what the
          relationship is, and that is the one thing here that is not compressible. */}
      {[
        { label: 'Routing platforms · works with', items: shippedRouters, cls: 'ev-grid-3' },
        { label: 'Direct providers · works with', items: shippedDirect, cls: 'ev-grid-5' },
      ].map((row) => (
        <div class="ev-cov-row">
          <p class="ev-cov-label">{row.label}</p>
          <ul class={`ev-grid ${row.cls}`}>
            {row.items.map((a) => (
              <li class="ev-obj ev-cell">
                {a.mark && (
                  <img class="ev-cell-mark" src={`/logos/providers/${a.mark}`} alt=""
                       width="26" height="26" loading="lazy" />
                )}
                <p class="ev-cell-name">{cellName(a.name)}</p>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </div>
      ))}

      {/* One line, no container and no label. Every other section closes on a bordered footer;
          this one has two small facts to add and a block around them made the section end on a
          second visual object. */}
      <p class="ev-src ev-cov-note">
        Coverage note: Bedrock currently carries gpt-oss routes only, and direct-provider prices
        are maintained here rather than read from each provider's API.
        {' '}<a href={GITHUB_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">See the route registry →</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <!-- ─── 7. GET STARTED ───
       One command, no paragraph. The old page waited until the eleventh screen to say how
       little is involved. -->
  <section class="section install" id="install">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="kicker">GET STARTED</p>
      <h2>Run it on your repository.</h2>

      <div class="terminal">
        <div class="terminal-bar" aria-hidden="true">
          <span class="dot r"></span>
          <span class="dot y"></span>
          <span class="dot g"></span>
          <span class="label">~/your-repo</span>
        </div>
        <div class="terminal-body">
          <span class="prompt">$</span>
          <code id="install-cmd">npx canaryone</code>
          <button class="copy" id="copy-btn" type="button" aria-label="Copy command">copy</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="hint">
        <a href={QUICKSTART} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the quickstart →</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <script is:inline>
    /* The install terminal's copy button, which is the home page's existing component. The
       only script on the page: the provider tree that needed measuring at runtime is gone. */
    (function () {
      const btn = document.getElementById('copy-btn');
      const cmd = document.getElementById('install-cmd');
      if (!btn || !cmd) return;
      btn.addEventListener('click', async () => {
        try {
          await navigator.clipboard.writeText(cmd.textContent.trim());
          btn.textContent = 'copied';
          btn.classList.add('copied');
          setTimeout(() => {
            btn.textContent = 'copy';
            btn.classList.remove('copied');
          }, 1600);
        } catch (e) {
          btn.textContent = 'select all';
          const sel = window.getSelection();
          const range = document.createRange();
          range.selectNodeContents(cmd);
          sel.removeAllRanges();
          sel.addRange(range);
          setTimeout(() => (btn.textContent = 'copy'), 2000);
        }
      });
    })();
  </script>
</Layout>

<style>
  /* Only what global.css has no equivalent for. The boundary diagram, the report frame, the
     terminal, the data table and every coverage note above are existing primitives. */

  /* ---- hero ---- */
  /* .hero .ctas is scoped to the home page's hero, so the band needs its own row. Same shape:
     the primary button and one ghost beside it, wrapping on a phone. */
  .ev-ctas {
